A restaurant-quality celebration of wholesome Italian garden cooking. This hearty minestrone builds layers of flavor starting with an aromatic soffritto of onions, carrots, and celery, followed by tender green beans, zucchini, kidney beans, and fire-roasted tomatoes simmered with Italian herbs. Small pasta shells cook directly in the broth, which is finished with tender baby spinach and nutty Parmesan.

*Adapted from [Carlsbad Cravings](https://carlsbadcravings.com/minestrone-soup/).*

## Ingredients

### Sauté Base & Vegetables
- 1 tablespoon extra-virgin olive oil
- 1 tablespoon unsalted butter
- 1 small yellow onion, finely diced
- 2 medium carrots, peeled and sliced (about 1 cup)
- 3 stalks celery, sliced (about 1 cup)
- 5 cloves garlic, minced
- 5 oz fresh green beans, trimmed and snapped into 1-inch lengths
- 1 medium zucchini, quartered lengthwise and sliced ¼ inch thick

### Broth, Beans & Seasonings
- 2 cans (14.5 oz each) fire-roasted diced tomatoes, undrained
- 1 can (15 oz) tomato sauce
- 1 can (15.5 oz) dark red kidney beans (or cannellini beans), rinsed and drained
- 1 ½ teaspoons dried basil
- 1 ½ teaspoons dried parsley
- 1 teaspoon dried oregano
- 1 teaspoon kosher salt
- 1 teaspoon granulated sugar (counterbalances tomato acid)
- ½ teaspoon freshly cracked black pepper
- ½ teaspoon dried thyme
- ¼ teaspoon crushed red pepper flakes
- 1 dried bay leaf
- 6 cups low-sodium chicken broth (or vegetable broth)
- 2 cups water

### Pasta & Fresh Greens
- 1 ½ cups uncooked small pasta shells (or ditalini or small elbow macaroni)
- 3 cups fresh baby spinach leaves, packed
- Freshly grated Parmigiano-Reggiano cheese (for serving)
- Fresh basil leaves, torn (optional, for garnish)

## Instructions

1. **Sauté the Soffritto:** Set a 6-to-7-quart enameled Dutch oven or heavy soup pot over medium heat. Add the olive oil and butter. Once melted and bubbling, dump in the diced onion, sliced carrots, and celery. Cook, stirring frequently, for 5 to 7 minutes until the vegetables soften and the onion becomes sweet and translucent. Stir in the minced garlic and cook for 30 seconds until fragrant.
2. **Assemble the Broth Base:** Add the fresh green beans, sliced zucchini, fire-roasted diced tomatoes with all their canning juices, tomato sauce, rinsed kidney beans, dried basil, parsley, oregano, kosher salt, sugar, black pepper, thyme, red pepper flakes, bay leaf, broth, and water. Stir thoroughly to disperse the herbs.
3. **Simmer the Vegetables:** Cover the Dutch oven with its lid and bring to a boil over medium-high heat. Once boiling, remove the lid, reduce the heat to medium-low, and maintain a gentle simmer for 10 minutes so the carrots and green beans begin to turn tender.
4. **Boil the Pasta in Broth:** Add the dry pasta shells directly into the simmering soup. Cook uncovered for 8 to 10 minutes, stirring occasionally so pasta doesn't settle to the bottom of the pot, until the pasta is cooked al dente.
5. **Wilt Spinach and Finish:** Pull the pot off the heat. Fish out and discard the bay leaf. Drop in the fresh baby spinach and stir gently; the residual heat will wilt the greens in about 60 seconds into a vibrant green ribbon.
6. **Ladle and Garnish:** Taste the broth and adjust salt and pepper if desired. Ladle into shallow soup bowls and top generously with freshly grated Parmesan cheese and torn fresh basil. Serve with warm crusty garlic bread.

## Notes

- **Pasta Tip for Leftovers:** If you plan to freeze this soup or eat it over several days, consider boiling the pasta separately and adding it to individual bowls when serving. Pasta left sitting in the refrigerator soup pot will continue soaking up broth.
- **Vegetable Swaps:** Swap kidney beans for cannellini or navy beans, or substitute chopped Tuscan kale or Swiss chard for the spinach.
